STRTOD(3)                      P32W Unix                       STRTOD(3)

NAME
       strtod, atof - convert a string to a double

SYNOPSIS
       #include <stdlib.h>

       double strtod(const char *s, char **end);
       double atof(const char *s);

DESCRIPTION
       strtod skips white space, then reads a decimal number (an
       optional sign, digits with an optional point, an optional
       exponent e or E with a sign), a hexadecimal one (0x1.8p3),
       inf, infinity or nan (any case; nan(...) too), and returns
       its value correctly rounded to the nearest double (ties to
       even): the same bits as any C library that rounds exactly.
       *end, if end is not NULL, is set past what was read, or to s
       when there is no number (and 0 is returned).

       A value too large gives +-HUGE_VAL (infinity) and errno
       ERANGE; one that rounds to zero or to a subnormal also sets
       ERANGE (as on macOS).

       atof(s) is strtod(s, NULL).

RETURN VALUE
       The value converted; 0 if there was no number.

EXAMPLES
       double v = strtod(argv[1], &end);
       if (end == argv[1]) fprintf(stderr, "not a number\n");

SEE ALSO
       atoi(3), strtoll(3), scanf(3), printf(3), math(3)

BUGS
       The conversion keeps 800 significant digits; any more only
       count as a sticky digit (the result is still correctly
       rounded). There are no locales: the point is always '.'.
